A||||2||JohnF||Diverters||||1||
Z||000000||JohnF||07-27-2001||04:23 PM||||Diverters apparently removed by recall 88-C-23 dealing primarily with engine fires.   Diverter was installed between forward edge of deck lid and rear glass to keep water and debris off forward portion of engine.  New diverters now available.  Opinions please.  Still no good?  Adds compartment temp?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||07-27-2001||04:41 PM||||I think everyone is going to have their own opinion on this one.  I totally removed mine...Pontiac just sliced the weatherstripping part of the diverter off it.<P>I think half of the reason the firewall exhaust manifolds crack is because water pours on it!  Since it also lets leaves and stuff fall down there wich is good for a fire.<P>I am 80% sure I am going to put some sort of weatherstipping back there.  But since my car is going to be good-weather only...maybe not.<P>Joe<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-27-2001||08:28 PM||||Water dumping on a hot manifold breaks them in short order. The "diverter" does not prevent this. I know, Mine is still there...<P>The main culprit is opening a wet deck lid over a hot engine. You may as well pour a bucket on it.<P>You get more down the grates than what the diverter will keep out at other times. With the lid shut and no diverter the water isn't likely to be a problem.<P>Leaves? I wouldn't worry about them much unless you have to park under trees like pine with needles or tiny leaves. I park right under a maple tree. I get some seeds down there in spring but it's not bad.<P>Would putting on back on hurt it? hard to say. probably not. I can't imagine the extra crack moves enough air to make any thermal diferance.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||1
